<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Msiri</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1830–<a href="/years/1891">1891</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Mwenda Msiri</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Msiri was a Nyamwezi trader from western Tanzania who founded the Yeke kingdom in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/katanga">Katanga</a> in the second half of the 19th century. Arriving around 1856, he built a state at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bunkeya">Bunkeya</a> based on trade in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> and slaves, at the expense of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazembe">Kazembe</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luba-empire">Luba</a>. He was shot dead in December 1891 by a member of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-free-state">Congo Free State</a> expedition under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-stairs">William Stairs</a>, which then occupied Katanga.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/msiri.jpg" alt="Msiri" width="320" height="362" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CAPELLO &amp; IVENS (H. - R.) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Msiri_1830_1891.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Msiri, whose full name was Mwenda Msiri Ngelengwa Shitambi, was a Nyamwezi from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabora">Tabora</a>. Like his father Kalasa he traded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> and slaves along the routes controlled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> of Zanzibar and his Arab and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swahili">Swahili</a> agents, which ran by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ujiji">Ujiji</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-tanganyika">Lake Tanganyika</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-mweru">Lake Mweru</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/katanga">Katanga</a>. He saw that guns were the key to power and that Katanga's copper and ivory could buy them. He formed a militia, conquered his neighbours and married into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luba-empire">Luba</a> royal family.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Because supplies from the east coast passed through his rivals' lands, Msiri sent his nephew Molenga west to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ovimbundu">Ovimbundu</a> and to Portuguese traders near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benguela">Benguela</a>, and a trader called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coimbra">Coimbra</a> became his supplier. He thus traded with both coasts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and dealt almost as an equal with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tippu-tip">Tippu Tip</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mirambo">Mirambo</a>. In 1870 he joined Tippu Tip in killing Mwata Kazembe, and he then influenced the choice of Kazembe's successors. He held his subject chiefs through his more than 500 wives, taking one from each chief's village to serve as a spy and, if needed, a hostage.</p><p>European sources describe his rule as cruel, with mutilations and executions, while the missionary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-stanley-arnot">Frederick Stanley Arnot</a>, whom he invited to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bunkeya">Bunkeya</a> and who arrived in 1886, called him a thorough gentleman.</p><p>In 1890 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-sharpe">Alfred Sharpe</a> brought a British protectorate treaty, which Msiri rejected once the missionary Charles Swan had it translated. In 1891 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-free-state">Congo Free State</a> expeditions led by Paul Le Marinel and then by Delcommune also failed to win his submission.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 20 December 1891 Lieutenant Omer Bodson of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stairs-expedition-to-katanga">Stairs Expedition</a> confronted Msiri at Munema and shot him; Bodson was himself mortally wounded. The expedition cut off Msiri's head, and its fate is unknown. The askaris massacred many of his people at Munema, and the population of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bunkeya">Bunkeya</a>, estimated at 60,000 to 80,000 in 1891, fell within a year to 10,000 to 20,000. His descendants keep the Garanganze chieftaincy at Bunkeya under the name Mwami Mwenda.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Msiri” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In Katanga, contested with Cecil Rhodes’s British South Africa Company, an expedition under William Stairs reached the Yeke capital in December 1891, and the Yeke king <b>Msiri</b> was shot dead.</blockquote><a href="/empire/congo-free-state">Congo Free State · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>At the same time, Swahili-Arab and Nyamwezi traders from the east, among them <b>Msiri</b>, whose Garanganze state rose in Katanga, together with Ovimbundu and Chokwe caravans from the west, stepped up the slave raiding and trading that tore at Luba social cohesion.</blockquote><a href="/empire/luba-empire">Luba Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In the late 19th century <b>Msiri's</b> Yeke state took Katanga and Swahili-Arab traders eroded Kazembe's power.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-kazembe">Kingdom of Kazembe</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Msiri</b>, a Nyamwezi trader from western Tanzania, arrived in Katanga around 1856.</blockquote><a href="/state/yeke-kingdom">Yeke Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Katanga Province, in what is now <a href="/countries/democratic-republic-of-the-congo">the Democratic Republic of the Congo</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-9,26&amp;year=1869" aria-label="Katanga Province on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5268 2500 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Msiri"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5718 2770h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5718 2770h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=-9,26&amp;year=1869">What was here in 1869? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
